# Bobby Plan Skill

> This skill handles planning — both epic breakdown and ticket refinement.

## Before Starting

1. **Check learnings** — Read `<%= paths.skills %>/bobby-plan/learnings.md` + `<%= paths.skills %>/bobby-plan/learnings.local.md`
2. **Read the ticket** — Understand what's being asked

## Investigation First

<investigation_rules>
Before creating any plan:
- Read the ticket's title, description, and acceptance criteria fully
- If the ticket has a `parent` field, read the parent epic's ticket.md and plan.md
- If the ticket has a `feature` field, read `.bobby/product/feature-map.md` (that row and the journey step(s) it serves) and `.bobby/product/personas.md` — the AC must satisfy the journey step, and test-cases.md should exercise it as the persona
- Read any existing `feature-plan.md` to understand cross-cutting decisions already made
- If you are unsure about the scope of an AC, improve the AC text rather than guessing
- Explore the codebase to understand existing patterns before proposing new ones
- **Read the code as it is on origin/main, not the checkout.** `git fetch origin` first, then read with `git show origin/main:<path>` or a worktree on origin/main. Local checkouts in a studio sit on old branches, and a plan built on them states wrong facts (10 of 24 build deviations traced back to this). Put the SHA you planned against at the top of `plan.md`.
- **Large file discipline:** For files over 500 lines (CSS, large components, mockups), do NOT read the entire file. Instead, grep for the relevant section name or keyword to find the line range, then read only that range. Record useful line ranges in the plan's `## Files to modify` section so downstream agents (build, review) can do targeted reads too.
- Problems you find that the ACs don't need: file them, don't widen the ACs or the plan to absorb them: `bobby ticket create -t "<what is wrong>" --type bug -p <priority> -a bobby-plan --found-in {ID}`, then `bobby ticket comment <new ID> --by bobby-plan "<what you saw, where, why it matters>"`. Report `Filed: <ID> — <title>` lines.
</investigation_rules>

## Mode Detection

- If the ticket has `type: epic` AND `.bobby/product/feature-map.md` exists → **Product-Aware Epic Mode** (decompose against the locked definition)
- If the ticket has `type: epic` → **Epic Mode** (break down into child tickets)
- If the ticket is in `planning` stage AND has a `parent` field (belongs to an epic) → **Feature-Aware Refine Mode** (plan with sibling context)
- If the ticket is in `planning` stage with no `parent` → **Refine Mode** (create plan + test cases)

Then read **only** that mode's file and follow it. Do not read the other modes:

| Mode | File |
|---|---|
| Product-Aware Epic Mode | `<%= paths.skills %>/bobby-plan/modes/product-epic.md` |
| Epic Mode | `<%= paths.skills %>/bobby-plan/modes/epic.md` |
| Feature-Aware Refine Mode | `<%= paths.skills %>/bobby-plan/modes/feature-refine.md` |
| Refine Mode | `<%= paths.skills %>/bobby-plan/modes/refine.md` |

## Size the ticket

After investigating and before planning, size the ticket and record it: `bobby ticket update {ID} --size <xs|s|m|l|xl>`. The size picks the pipeline (xs runs plan → build → test with no separate review; s to l run the full workflow; a security-sensitive ticket stays on `secure` whatever its size) and how much plan to write.

| Size | Looks like | Plan to write |
|---|---|---|
| xs | Copy, config or a one-file change with no new behavior path | Goal, files, test cases. No approach scoring. |
| s | One repo, a few files, one behavior | Approach in two lines, files, steps, test cases |
| m | Several files or both repos, a new behavior | Full plan; a `## Contract` section if it crosses API and UI |
| l | Cross-cutting: several new paths, migrations, both repos | Full plan, `## Contract`, rollout and risks, a checkpoint per acceptance criterion |
| xl | More than one shippable thing, or more than about six acceptance criteria | Do not plan it as one ticket. Switch to Epic Mode: create the child tickets, make this the epic. |

A plan longer than its ticket needs costs every later stage that reads it. Size honestly: when unsure between two sizes, take the larger.

## Spec trace (any ticket with a design spec or a parent spec)

When the ticket or its epic has a design spec (`design-spec.md`, `spec.md`, a design folder), the plan answers to it, not just to the ticket:

- Add a `## Spec trace` section: each spec requirement this ticket covers, and the plan step that implements it.
- Anything the spec asks for that the plan leaves out or changes goes under `## Deviations from spec`, with why. A deviation that removes behavior the spec names (the template's own field, a control, a state) needs the human's OK: `bobby ticket move {ID} block "Spec deviation: <what and why>"`. "Never uses Google Places" against a spec that says "the template's own field" is exactly this.
- **Open items block.** If the spec marks something this ticket depends on as unconfirmed ("to confirm", "open item", "TBD"), don't plan around it: block with the decision needed. A value nobody confirmed gets built, tested and passed as if it were decided.
- "The template's own X" or "the live page's X" means reuse that component and its behavior, not copy its markup.

## Contract (any ticket that crosses API and UI, or uses another ticket's output)

The largest single cause of rejected builds is code written against an assumed or stubbed data shape. When the work reads or writes data across a boundary, add a `## Contract` section to `plan.md`:

- the exact request and response (JSON with real field names and types), copied from the serializer, controller or fixture on origin/main, with the file and line it came from;
- for a shape that does not exist yet, the shape this ticket or its sibling will produce, named as such, and the ticket that owns it.

`test-cases.md` and the build's fixtures use this shape. In an epic, copy it into `feature-plan.md` so sibling tickets build against the same thing.

**Lists kept in sync by hand.** If the work adds to, or depends on, a value mirrored by hand in two places (a registry twinned across repos, route names that must stay off a reserved list, an enum in the API and the UI), name the pair in the plan and add a test that fails when they drift apart. Handles colliding with site pages happened because two such lists had no test.

